{"results":[{"url":"https://www.facebook.com/thetelegraphindia/videos/comedian-kunal-kamra-took-a-political-jibe-at-the-centre-saying-the-government-i/4549666955264352/","final_url":"https://www.facebook.com/thetelegraphindia/videos/comedian-kunal-kamra-took-a-political-jibe-at-the-centre-saying-the-government-i/4549666955264352/","title":"12K views · 264 reactions | Comedian Kunal Kamra took a political jibe at the Centre, saying the government is \"taking Sita's husband's name and doing Nita's husband's work.\" The remark was made before protesters at Jantar Mantar where activist Sonam Wangchuk is on a hunger strike for past 19 days. | The Telegraph","description":"Comedian Kunal Kamra took a political jibe at the Centre, saying the government is \"taking Sita's husband's name and doing Nita's husband's work.\" The remark was made before protesters at Jantar...","language":"en","text":"An encryption key that was supposed to be practically impossible to crack turned out to be… 4.\nNo, that's not a joke.\nEight cryptographic keys belonging to Indian digital-trust company eMudhra were found to be dangerously weak. And security researchers demonstrated just how easily they could be recovered.\nBut why should you care?\nBecause these are the very systems that help your browser decide whether the website you're visiting is actually legitimate.\nCould someone have impersonated your bank's website? Could malicious software have passed itself off as legitimate?\nAnd how did a flaw this basic survive for around 18 months, including two independent audit cycles?\nWe break down the eMudhra cybersecurity incident, what it means for ordinary internet users, and why the bigger problem is the trust we place in the invisible infrastructure of the internet.\nWatch the full explainer.\n#eMudhra #CyberSecurity #InternetSecurity #DataPrivacy #DigitalIndia #TechExplained #CyberSafety","author":null,"published_date":null,"latency_ms":3879.135713999858,"format":"markdown"}],"errors":[]}